### Why does a rolling deployment hurt more than expected?

The core reason is the default eager rebalance protocol. On any membership change, every consumer revokes all of its partitions, the coordinator recomputes the assignment, and every consumer re-joins. During a rolling deployment, each pod stop and start triggers a separate membership change, so a group of N pods pays roughly 2N stop-the-world rebalances.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class RebalanceCostCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int pods = 6;
        int rebalancesPerPod = 2; // one for stop, one for start
        int total = pods * rebalancesPerPod;
        int pausePerRebalanceMs = 5000; // typical eager rebalance
        int totalPauseMs = total * pausePerRebalanceMs;

        System.out.println("Rolling deploy of " + pods + " pods:");
        System.out.println("  Eager rebalances: " + total);
        System.out.println("  Pause per rebalance: " + pausePerRebalanceMs + "ms");
        System.out.println("  Total group pause: " + totalPauseMs + "ms (" + totalPauseMs/1000 + "s)");
        System.out.println("  During each pause: NO partition in the group is consumed");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Rolling deploy of 6 pods:
  Eager rebalances: 12
  Pause per rebalance: 5000ms
  Total group pause: 60000ms (60s)
  During each pause: NO partition in the group is consumed
```

### How does the cooperative sticky assignor fix this?

With cooperative rebalancing, consumers only revoke the specific partitions that actually need to move, and they keep consuming everything else throughout.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class CooperativeStickyConfig {
    public static Properties create() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");

        // Switch from eager to cooperative sticky
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.PARTITION_ASSIGNMENT_STRATEGY_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor");

        System.out.println("Cooperative sticky assignor enabled");
        System.out.println("  Only partitions that need to move are paused");
        System.out.println("  Other partitions keep consuming throughout");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        create();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Cooperative sticky assignor enabled
  Only partitions that need to move are paused
  Other partitions keep consuming throughout
```

### How does static group membership eliminate rebalances?

With a static member, you give each pod a stable identity via `group.instance.id`. When that pod restarts within `session.timeout.ms`, the coordinator recognizes it as the same member returning and skips the rebalance entirely.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class StaticMembershipConfig {
    public static Properties create(String podOrdinal) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");

        // Static membership: stable identity per pod
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_INSTANCE_ID_CONFIG,
            "settlement-consumer-" + podOrdinal);

        // Session timeout must exceed pod restart time
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS_CONFIG, "60000"); // 60s

        // Cooperative sticky for remaining rebalances
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.PARTITION_ASSIGNMENT_STRATEGY_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor");

        System.out.println("Static member: settlement-consumer-" + podOrdinal);
        System.out.println("  Quick restart -> zero rebalances");
        System.out.println("  Session timeout: 60s (exceeds restart time)");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // In Kubernetes, derive ordinal from StatefulSet pod name
        create("3"); // settlement-consumer-3
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Static member: settlement-consumer-3
  Quick restart -> zero rebalances
  Session timeout: 60s (exceeds restart time)
```

### How do eager and cooperative rebalancing compare?

| Aspect | Eager (Default) | Cooperative Sticky |
|---|---|---|
| On membership change | All consumers revoke all partitions | Only affected partitions revoke |
| Group pause | Full stop-the-world | Only moved partitions pause |
| Rolling deploy cost | 2N full pauses | 2N minimal pauses |
| Partition affinity | Partitions reshuffled | Returning pod gets old partitions |
| Cache warming | Lost on every rebalance | Preserved (sticky) |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class RebalanceComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("6-pod rolling deploy comparison:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Eager rebalance:");
        System.out.println("  12 stop-the-world pauses");
        System.out.println("  All 12 partitions paused each time");
        System.out.println("  Caches cold after every rebalance");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cooperative sticky + static membership:");
        System.out.println("  0 rebalances (pod restarts within session timeout)");
        System.out.println("  Partitions never paused");
        System.out.println("  Caches stay warm (sticky assignment)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
6-pod rolling deploy comparison:

Eager rebalance:
  12 stop-the-world pauses
  All 12 partitions paused each time
  Caches cold after every rebalance

Cooperative sticky + static membership:
  0 rebalances (pod restarts within session timeout)
  Partitions never paused
  Caches stay warm (sticky assignment)
```

### How do you ensure graceful shutdown?

Make sure the pod's `preStop` hook calls `consumer.close()` so the consumer leaves the group gracefully rather than timing out.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;

public class GracefulShutdown {
    public static void registerShutdownHook(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer) {
        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Graceful shutdown: closing consumer");
            consumer.close(); // Leaves group cleanly
            System.out.println("Consumer closed -- group notified immediately");
        }));
    }
}
```

### What about Kubernetes StatefulSet configuration?

In Kubernetes, derive the pod ordinal from the StatefulSet pod name for the `group.instance.id`.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class KubernetesOrdinal {
    public static String getPodOrdinal() {
        String hostname = System.getenv("HOSTNAME"); // e.g., settlement-2
        if (hostname != null && hostname.contains("-")) {
            String ordinal = hostname.substring(hostname.lastIndexOf('-') + 1);
            System.out.println("Pod hostname: " + hostname);
            System.out.println("Ordinal: " + ordinal);
            return ordinal;
        }
        return "0";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String ordinal = getPodOrdinal();
        String instanceId = "settlement-consumer-" + ordinal;
        System.out.println("group.instance.id: " + instanceId);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Pod hostname: settlement-2
Ordinal: 2
group.instance.id: settlement-consumer-2
```

### What are common mistakes when fixing rebalance storms?

The most common mistake is enabling cooperative sticky but forgetting to set a stable `group.instance.id`, so every restart still triggers a rebalance. Another error is setting `session.timeout.ms` shorter than the pod restart time, which causes the coordinator to evict the static member before it returns. A third mistake is mixing eager and cooperative assignors in the same group during migration, which causes a protocol mismatch.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class RebalanceMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: cooperative sticky without static membership");
        System.out.println("  partition.assignment.strategy=CooperativeStickyAssignor");
        System.out.println("  // No group.instance.id -> still triggers rebalance");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: session timeout too short");
        System.out.println("  group.instance.id=settlement-1");
        System.out.println("  session.timeout.ms=10000  // Pod takes 30s to restart");
        System.out.println("  // Coordinator evicts before pod returns!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: cooperative + static + generous timeout");
        System.out.println("  CooperativeStickyAssignor + group.instance.id");
        System.out.println("  session.timeout.ms=60000 (exceeds restart time)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: cooperative sticky without static membership
  partition.assignment.strategy=CooperativeStickyAssignor
  // No group.instance.id -> still triggers rebalance

WRONG: session timeout too short
  group.instance.id=settlement-1
  session.timeout.ms=10000  // Pod takes 30s to restart
  // Coordinator evicts before pod returns!

CORRECT: cooperative + static + generous timeout
  CooperativeStickyAssignor + group.instance.id
  session.timeout.ms=60000 (exceeds restart time)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

These two fixes stack. Static membership removes rebalances for quick restarts, and the cooperative assignor minimizes the cost of rebalances that remain. The sticky assignor returns a pod to its old partitions, so warmed caches are preserved. Tune `session.timeout.ms` to comfortably exceed your pod's restart time. Measure the rebalance count during a deploy before and after the fix.
